WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.500
Love it or hate it, there's never been a film
quite like "Space Jam: A New Legacy."

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.080
Set in the Warner Bros. "ServerVerse," it's
packed with references that will go over the

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.120
heads of younger viewers.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.609
These are the things only adults will notice
in "Space Jam: A New Legacy."

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:22.560
Sure, LeBron James has his name above the
"Space Jam" sequel's title, much like Michael

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.619
Jordan did in the original "Space Jam."

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:30.859
But, also like that 1996 film, the villain
team is composed of mutated versions of basketball

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:31.859
superstars.

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:35.969
This time around, rather than the Monstars,
we get the Goon Squad.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:42.210
This team includes both NBA and WNBA star
players, but with some significant upgrades.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.940
"Okay, let's analyze the competition!"

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.579
Klay Thompson becomes "Wet-Fire," a Goon who
has aquakinesis (the ability to control water)

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:54.210
and pyrokinesis (the ability to manipulate
fire).

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.870
The nature of the character is a reference
to the "Splash Brothers," a moniker given

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:03.160
to Thompson and Stephen Curry because of their
ability to "splash" the ball with three-point

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:08.340
shots, and a riff on the old "Bash Brothers"
nickname bestowed upon '80s baseball superstars

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.140
Jose Canseco and Mark McGwire.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.090
Anthony Davis is the Brow, a winged creature
with a unibrow.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:20.330
Also based on a real nickname, Davis has gained
notoriety not only for his skills on the court,

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.720
but for, well, his unibrow.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.920
Diana Taurasi appears as White Mamba, a snake-like
being.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:32.770
The character name is a reference to one the
late Kobe Bryant bestowed upon her years ago,

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.560
praising her skills on the court by riffing
off his own "Black Mamba" moniker.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.810
Damian Lillard becomes Chronos, perhaps the
most powerful of the Goon Squad.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.079
He's basically an android who can speed up
and slow down time to his liking.

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:51.240
The character's inspiration comes from "Dame
Time," a phrase that has followed Lillard

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.219
since he was 13, referring to his ability
to stay calm under pressure and seemingly

00:01:56.219 --> 00:02:01.369
control time as he casually blows past the
competition.

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:07.829
Finally, WNBA Sparks forward Nneka Ogwumike
is the scary Arachnneka, a spider-like Goon

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:08.929
with six arms.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:14.569
There are a ton of jokes in "Legacy" about
LeBron's near-constant movement from one basketball

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:15.569
team to the next.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:21.560
Since his 2003 debut, despite being a four-time
Most Valuable Player and 17-time all-star,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.211
he has played for the Cleveland Cavaliers,
the Miami Heat, the Cleveland Cavaliers (again),

00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:32.090
and now the Los Angeles Lakers — who, as
Don Cheadle's Al G. Rhythm jokes, better not

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:33.740
get too used to him.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:38.790
Bugs' chosen nickname for King James is "Cleveland,"
not only a reference to his Ohio-upbringing

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:44.090
but also the city that has loved (and hated)
and loved (and hated) him as he has come and

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.700
gone over the years.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.920
The funny thing is, at one time LeBron planned
to use a "Legacy" announcement to break Cleveland's

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.280
heart once again.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:57.450
In 2018, as the world waited to see where
the free agent would land next, a teaser trailer

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:02.370
was prepared that reportedly had the Looney
Tunes characters stealing sports memorabilia,

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.959
with LeBron retrieving the memorabilia and
then appearing in full Lakers gear.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:12.459
It would have served the dual purpose of teasing
the film and announcing James' new home — but

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:14.590
was ultimately scuttled at the last minute.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.599
There's a good chance your child may have
seen the original "Space Jam," and if so there's

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.260
also a good chance they remember the name
Michael Jordan.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.519
That would help with the funniest scene of
"A New Legacy," when Sylvester the cat announces

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.599
at halftime that the lopsided score is okay,
because he just found another basketball great

00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:35.780
to join the team — none other than Michael
Jordan.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:40.580
After watching the lower half of a mysterious
figure enter the room to great anticipation,

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:45.329
the reveal is that the toons have accidentally
recruited Michael B. Jordan — the actor

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:46.660
— to help out.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:51.129
He looks confused, a lot of popcorn gets tossed
around, and then he heads back to his seat

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:53.300
to watch the conclusion of the game.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.860
It's a funny joke, but you can already imagine
parents all over the world saying "You know,

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:58.930
from 'Black Panther!'

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.129
The 'Creed' movies?

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:01.849
Um, 'Fantastic Four?'"

00:04:01.849 --> 00:04:05.280
Oh well, someday they'll grow up and find
it really funny.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.310
Early in the film, we flashback to Ohio in
the '90s, when a young LeBron learns the value

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:15.920
of hard work on the court — and of not wasting
his time on video games.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.790
It's a cruel lesson, but one with a basis
in reality.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:23.690
In real life, the now primitive-looking but
beloved-at-the-time Game Boy was all the rage

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.260
from its introduction in 1989 until the late
'90s.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:33.250
The game we see LeBron playing is 1990's "Bugs
Bunny Crazy Castle," which featured the titular

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:38.070
Looney Tune scampering up ladders and being
sucked through tunnels Mario-style while grabbing

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.080
carrots and avoiding baddies.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.350
Decades later, perhaps basketball fans can
be thankful that LeBron put away his childish

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:46.350
things.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.450
But still, tossing a Game Boy in the trash?

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:53.320
In the '90s, such behavior would've been viewed
by most kids as nothing short of a criminal

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.320
act.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.100
If you see "Legacy" with a child, don't be
surprised if they have lots of questions about

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.070
all the characters in the background.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:06.060
As Al G. Rhythm explains, the crowd for the
big basketball game is made up of half "everyday

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.290
people" sucked into the ServerVerse by LeBron
James' social media, and half "iconic Warner

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:15.040
Bros. characters" who make up their own WB
universe.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.000
The result is one of the coolest things about
the movie — if admittedly, done before in

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.560
movies and TV shows like "Ready Player One"
and South Park's "Imaginationland" trilogy.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.370
"Count Chocula, Cinderella, Snarf from 'Thundercats.'"

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:31.910
The background of the final act is populated
by characters from "The Wizard of Oz," "Game

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.470
of Thrones," and even the violent Droogs from
"A Clockwork Orange."

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:41.220
If your kid catches the reference to the nun
from Ken Russell's 1971 trippy 17th-century

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.310
nightmare flick "The Devils," well, consider
us impressed.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.100
There are also lots of WB cartoon characters
in the background of those basketball game

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.810
scenes — so kids should have no problem
identifying them, right?

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:55.810
Think again.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:01.460
It seems highly unlikely that a kid in the
year 2021 is going to geek out over the Herculoids

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:07.530
— but protoplasmic creatures Gloop and Gleep
from the 1967 series are right there, cheering

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.910
on the Tune Squad in multiple scenes.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:14.720
Peter Potamus can be seen floating above the
court in his magic flying balloon, flanked

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.570
by the likes of Space Ghost, Jabberjaw, the
Great Gazoo, Magilla Gorilla, Captain Caveman,

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:26.050
and others that kids most likely won't recognize
unless they're allowed to watch Adult Swim.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.870
If they're hip, they might recognize "Rick
and Morty"; if they watched the 2020 Animaniacs

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.930
reboot on Hulu, they might be delighted to
spot Yakko, Wakko, and Dot hanging around.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:41.560
But if they don't recognize the Scooby-Doo
gang and their Mystery Machine, well, it sounds

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.480
like your family has some cartoons to watch.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.340
One fun element of "Legacy" is that Bugs and
LeBron must put together their team by roaming

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.800
the ServerVerse, finding Looney Tunes characters
who reside on planets themed around classic

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.310
WB movies.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:01.990
To an adult, it's a fun detour — to a kid,
some explanation may be required.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:07.550
The duo visit a DC-themed planet, where Superman
and other Justice League heroes are glimpsed

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:12.190
as Daffy Duck and Porky Pig are recruited
— most kids, one would think, would understand

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:13.270
those references.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:19.560
After that, however, they enter the 2015 blockbuster
"Mad Max: Fury Road," which earned a hard

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:24.120
"R" rating and most likely isn't in the Saturday
morning rotation for kids.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.830
The recruitment team also travels to a black-and-white
"Casablanca" world, where "Play it again,

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.020
Sam" takes on a whole new meaning.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:36.090
Although a cartoon Humphrey Bogart appeared
alongside Bugs in "8 Ball Bunny," the legendary

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.210
actor doesn't appear in this version.

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:43.190
While seeing such prominent recognition of
the 1942 movie is wonderful, the scene might

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:47.180
soar over the heads of kids like LeBron going
for a dunk.

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:51.400
Perhaps most enjoyable is a visit to a "Matrix"
planet, where Granny and Tweety have become

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.880
a couple of bullet-dodging badasses.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:58.880
Then there's the Austin Powers planet, where
Elmer Fudd has become a Mini-Me of Dr. Evil

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:03.710
and poor Sylvester has been shaven to become
the villain's new hairless pet cat.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:10.080
As an adult, you might be jazzed to spot Wood
Harris, aka Avon Barksdale from "The Wire,"

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.140
playing LeBron's no-nonsense childhood coach.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.390
But chances are, the children in your life
will be much more preoccupied looking for

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:18.390
Taz.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:23.040
Other adult-friendly cameos in "Legacy" include
Sarah Silverman, stifling her famously foul

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.900
mouth temporarily to portray a duplicitous
WB executive.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:33.000
Alongside Silverman is recent Oscar nominee
Steven Yeun as another eager exec.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:38.380
LeBron's wife is played by "Star Trek" and
"Walking Dead" series vet Sonequa Martin-Green.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.610
Then there are the basketball game announcers,
sucked in via their phones and told that unless

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:46.620
the Tune Squad wins, they'll become stuck
in the ServerVerse forever.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:52.960
The guy in the bowtie is smooth-voiced sportscaster
Ernie Johnson, and to his left is Lil Rel

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.380
Howery, a funny supporting actor perhaps best
known for "Get Out."

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:02.370
Also worth mentioning is Zendaya as the new
voice of Lola Bunny, and funnyman Gabriel

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:05.250
Iglesias vocalizing Speedy Gonzales.

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:11.060
Adult and child fans of the classic Looney
Tunes cartoons alike will be rewarded for

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:15.830
their hours spent before the boob tube, as
certain moments in "Legacy" recall classic

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:16.830
shorts.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:21.770
When LeBron first encounters Bugs Bunny, the
wascally wabbit shoves a hunter's cap and

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:26.090
a shotgun onto the basketball star, insisting
that he act out a scene.

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:31.400
LeBron goes through a few physical transformations
as he becomes a cartoon, one resembling the

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:37.580
"early version" of Elmer Fudd seen in cartoons
like 1942's "The Wacky Wabbit."

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:43.180
Bugs and LeBron also keep tearing "Rabbit
Season" and "Duck Season" signs off the tree,

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:47.681
re-creating one of the most iconic cartoon
sequences ever committed to film: the Bugs

00:09:47.681 --> 00:09:51.810
and Daffy square-off in 1951's "Rabbit Fire."

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:55.960
While LeBron attempts to explain his dilemma
to Bugs — his son Dom has been kidnapped

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:00.700
by Al G. Rhythm and he needs to recruit a
basketball team to get him back — Bugs puts

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:04.600
the basketball star through a series of torturous
cartoon moments.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.690
Perhaps the most recognizable is the barbershop
sequence from 1950's "The Rabbit of Seville",

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:13.820
with Bugs shaving and spinning his unwitting
victim in a barber chair.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.460
Lots of other classic Looney Tunes bits are
littered throughout the film, from Marvin

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:24.940
the Martian's "You have made me very angry!"
to Wile E. Coyote's backfiring Acme products.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:26.870
